<?xml version="1.0"?>
<events>
	<!-- ONAT: MULTI-SITE SURGE - ONE request (the live video of a match, VIDEO_APP = service 0) comes
	     from FOUR places at once, and only 4 UAVs host that service (SINGLE placement: UAV i hosts
	     service i % 4, so one starts in each quadrant). The question it asks of a UAV mobility
	     policy: where do the 4 VIDEO UAVs have to be to lose the fewest requests? A UAV serves about
	     1.25 requests/s (a 0.8 s task), so the 4 of them serve about 5 requests/s together.
	       - STADIUM_MAIN, the big event, in the middle of the map: a wide crowd (radius 150 m, wider
	         than the 100 m a UAV reaches, so one UAV cannot even cover all of it) asking ~2.4
	         requests/s at 800 users - about 2 UAVs' worth.
	       - FANZONE_NORTH / _SOUTHWEST / _SOUTHEAST, three small events on the edges, same request:
	         each a small crowd (radius 70 m - one UAV covers all of it) asking ~0.65-0.85 requests/s
	         at 800 users - over half a UAV each. They start a minute apart while the stadium is still
	         going (so UAVs have to leave it for the edges) and are over before it ends (so they have
	         to come back).
	     What to look for in the heat-map video (the sites have the same symbol on the timeline - same
	     request):
	       - 300-900 s: only the stadium. The VIDEO UAVs should gather there and spread over the crowd:
	         two of them cover and serve it, stacking all four on its center covers no more of it.
	       - 900-1420 s: all four sites at once. At 800 users they ask ~4.6 requests/s in total
	         against ~5 served, but that takes 2 + 1 + 1 + 1 UAVs and there are only 4, so some site has
	         to go short, and what separates the policies is where the shortage falls. A policy that
	         follows the biggest crowd leaves the fan zones with nobody in range (every request there
	         is rejected); one that sends a UAV to each site overloads the stadium (queues full,
	         requests rejected); one that leaves the VIDEO UAVs near where they started misses most.
	       - 1420-1500 s: the stadium alone again: the UAVs that went to the edges have to come back.
	     At fewer users the same schedule is a lighter test: at 200-500 users one UAV per site
	     (two on the stadium, for its width) is enough capacity, so the failures that remain are UAVs
	     that are late or in the wrong place, not queues. The whole 100-800 sweep reads as one curve
	     per policy.
	     The sites form a triangle around the stadium (417 m away, ~710 m from each other): a UAV needs
	     30-50 s to cross from one to the next at 15 m/s. The warm-up (180 s) is over before the
	     first event starts. All times in simulation seconds (1800 s in total, nothing after 1500 s). -->

	<event name="STADIUM_MAIN">
		<start_time>300</start_time>
		<end_time>1500</end_time>
		<x_pos>500</x_pos>
		<y_pos>500</y_pos>
		<radius>150</radius>
		<attraction_radius>350</attraction_radius>
		<participation>0.7</participation>
		<request>VIDEO_APP</request>
		<poisson_interarrival>100</poisson_interarrival>
	</event>

	<event name="FANZONE_NORTH">
		<start_time>900</start_time>
		<end_time>1300</end_time>
		<x_pos>500</x_pos>
		<y_pos>900</y_pos>
		<radius>70</radius>
		<attraction_radius>200</attraction_radius>
		<participation>0.8</participation>
		<request>VIDEO_APP</request>
		<poisson_interarrival>75</poisson_interarrival>
	</event>

	<event name="FANZONE_SOUTHWEST">
		<start_time>960</start_time>
		<end_time>1360</end_time>
		<x_pos>140</x_pos>
		<y_pos>290</y_pos>
		<radius>70</radius>
		<attraction_radius>200</attraction_radius>
		<participation>0.8</participation>
		<request>VIDEO_APP</request>
		<poisson_interarrival>75</poisson_interarrival>
	</event>

	<event name="FANZONE_SOUTHEAST">
		<start_time>1020</start_time>
		<end_time>1420</end_time>
		<x_pos>860</x_pos>
		<y_pos>290</y_pos>
		<radius>70</radius>
		<attraction_radius>200</attraction_radius>
		<participation>0.8</participation>
		<request>VIDEO_APP</request>
		<poisson_interarrival>75</poisson_interarrival>
	</event>
</events>
